Traditional adsorbent materials, such as clay minerals,6,12,32,33 nanosized metal oxides (NMOs),34,35 nanoscale zero-valent iron (NZVI),2,23–25 layered double hydroxides (LDHs),5,10,11 layered metal sulfides (LMS),36 activated carbon (AC),37,38 carbonaceous materials,14,39,40 ordered mesoporous carbon (OMC),41 organic resins,42,43 carbon nanotubes $\left(\mathbf{CNTS}\right)^{9,30,44,45}$ and graphene oxides $(\mathbf{GO})^{3,7,13,46,47}$ have been widely applied in the adsorption of toxic/ radioactive metal ions. However, these adsorbent materials generally suffer from one or several of the following disadvantages: (i) slow adsorption kinetics/limited selectivity (LDHs, NMOs and clays); (ii) small pore sizes or pore volumes (AC, carbonaceous materials); (iii) poor regeneration and reusability (organic resins); (iv) relatively low thermal, chemical and radiation stability (LMS); (v) relatively complicated preparation and small production scale (CNTs and GO); and (vi) low adsorption capacity. Therefore, the development of advanced porous materials for more effective and efficient adsorption is highly desirable to overcome existing limitations, and maximize the adsorption capacity with economic preparation methods and good recycling ability.
## 1.2 Brief introduction to MOFs
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline porous materials consisting of metal nodes (i.e. metal ions or clusters) and organic linkers connected via coordination bonds.48 It is well established that MOFs are one of the most widely investigated materials of the 21st century owing to their structural tailorability, controlled porosity, and high crystallinity.49,50 Widely used metal ions for the construction of MOFs include $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ , $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ , Ca(II), Al(III), Mg(II), $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ , Co(II), $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{\left(rv\right)}$ , $\operatorname{Ln}(\operatorname{III})$ , and $\operatorname{Ti}(\operatorname{III})$ , which can adopt various coordination geometries, such as trigonal bipyramidal, pyramidal, square, tetrahedral and octahedral.51 Some of these metal ions, such as $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ or $\mathrm{Eu(m)}$ and $\mathrm{\bfAg(\mathrm{_I)}}$ , are known to be toxic or expensive,1,52,53 while they are still helpful for understanding the basic chemistry of MOFs. Organic linkers, such as amines, carboxylates, sulfonates and phosphates, are key components for the synthesis of MOFs.54–57 Owing to the liability of metal nodes, coordination bonds formed between metal nodes and organic ligands are usually reversible, which ensures the formation of highly-ordered framework structures.58,59
MOFs have been studied for a wide variety of applications, such as gas storage, catalysis, drug delivery and luminescence sensing.61,68–74 Nowadays, many MOFs have shown excellent stability under harsh conditions.75–81 Additionally, MOFs can be synthesized on a large scale through low-priced and simple synthetic methods.82–86 The surface areas of typical MOFs range from 1000 to $10000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ,66 and exceed those of traditional porous materials such as zeolites and carbons. MOFs with permanent porosity are more extensive in their variety than any other class of porous materials. These advantageous characteristics together with their ordered porous structures, tunable physiochemical properties, and ability to be modified make MOFs highly promising next generation adsorbent materials with remarkably high adsorption efficiencies for toxic/radioactive metal ions in environmental pollution management. Over the last decade, a lot of researchers have reported the potential applications of MOFs and MOF-based composites for the removal of toxic77,78,87–90 and nuclear waste-related metal ions53,93,96–98 from wastewaters. Fig. 1 illustrates some developmental milestones of MOF-based materials for the adsorption of toxic and nuclear waste-related metal ions.
## 1.3 Strategies for boosting the adsorption performance of MOFs
The simple synthesis methods of MOFs (Fig. 2a) make their subsequent decoration highly convenient, and provide a facile way to design their structural complexity, composition uniformity, and reproducibility. The adsorption performances of MOFs, such as adsorption kinetics, adsorption capacity, adsorption thermodynamics, selectivity, stability, and recyclability, are controlled by their highly-ordered framework structures, hydrophilicity, surface area, functionality, and pore size and distribution. Approaches to improving the adsorption performance of MOFs have attracted considerable attention in recent years, and can be divided into five categories (Fig. 2b): (i) MOFs derived from large organic linkers. The porosity of MOFs (e.g. NU-1000, UiO-67 and UiO-68) can be enlarged by synthesis from longer organic linkers to obtain the parent MOF.75,77 This method is very powerful for enlarging the pore size of MOFs, facilitating the diffusion of metal ions through the MOFs.77 However, this approach depends on the commercial availability of the target organic linkers, which are often expensive; (ii) MOFs designed with defects. Specifically, adding modulators to the precursor mixture will create defects in the MOFs, which thus enlarge their pore size and introduce extra adsorption sites into MOFs.98–101
In addition, defects make MOFs more hydrophilic.102 For example, the formation of defects can be observed when UiO-66 is synthesized in the presence of monocarboxylic acid modulators under the most commonly used conditions.101 This approach is simpler and more economical than synthesizing MOFs with longer organic linkers; (iii) MOFs with functionalities in the organic linkers. Under traditional synthetic conditions, functional groups are introduced into MOFs with the use of an organic linker resembling that of the parent material, but with an additional, pendant functional groups (e.g., $\mathbf{-NH_{2}},^{77,103-105}\mathbf{-OH},^{77,106,107}$ and $-\mathrm{SH}^{78}$ ).
MOFs functionalized with specific functional groups can offer additional adsorption sites and also improve the selectivity of pristine MOFs; (iv) MOFs with functionalities at the metal nodes, that is MOFs prepared in the presence of a metal precursor modified by specific molecules.108,109 In terms of mercury adsorption, Liang et al. synthesized sulfur-functionalized MOFs using $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NCS})_{4}^{2-}$ , in which chemically soft S atom is available for the capture of heavy metal ions such as Hg(II).108 Post-synthetic ligand exchange and post-synthetic modification methods are widely used to introduce functional groups into MOFs.110–113 Generally, functional groups coordinated to the metal cluster are more active than those incorporated into the organic ligands because of steric hindrance; (v) MOF-based composites originating from the hybridization of MOFs with other functional materials, such as magnetic materials (e.g. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4,}$ ).114,115 Magnetic MOFs are advantageous for easy recovery from solutions under the application of a magnetic field; other adsorptive materials, such as MOF–GO composites,116 usually exhibit enhanced properties compared with their individual counterparts owing to the synergistic effects among different components.116,117 In addition, MOFs are usually isolated as small particles $\left(50-100\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ tending to form fine water suspensions that either move through the columns or result in column clogging. These drawbacks of MOFs can also be overcome by transforming them into composite forms.82,92
## 1.4 The purpose of this review
To date, a number of reviews and monographs have summarized the fundamental theory of MOFs as well as their synthesis and important physicochemical properties.58,60,69–71,74,118–120 The adsorption of hazardous organic pollutants such as industrially relevant toxic chemicals, some volatile organic compounds, and harmful gases onto MOFs and corresponding adsorption mechanisms have also been summarized thoroughly.121–127 However, there have been few reviews that have focused on toxic/radioactive metal ion adsorption onto such materials,128–132 and many of these articles only briefly discussed the adsorption performance of MOFs. It is still necessary to give a comprehensive summarization to the specific toxic and radioactive metal ion decontamination. In this review, we provide a systematic elaboration of the metal ion adsorption properties on MOFs and their composites, examining topics such as macroscopic batch experimental performance, microscopic spectroscopy analysis, and theoretical calculations. We place emphasis not only on the potential of MOFs in industrial applications but also techniques for characterizing their adsorption behaviours/mechanisms. The adsorption properties are assessed among MOF-based materials and compared with those of other widely used adsorbents. The MOFs themselves will not be discussed in detail, since there are many outstanding review articles on MOF-type materials and their other applications.58,60,69–71,74 We believe that offering a focused review to summarize the emerging applications of MOF-based materials to toxic/radioactive metal ion adsorption is of great importance for future development of metal ion decontamination. This will help researchers to obtain a deep understanding of the interactions between MOFs and metal ions, as well as strategies for the design and synthesis of MOFbased materials as high-performance adsorbents for environmental pollution management.
## 2. Techniques involved in analysing the adsorption behaviours of MOFbased materials
Rapid development in the application of MOFs as adsorbents has generated interest in characterizing the interactions between MOFs and toxic/radioactive metal ions. Main adsorption mechanisms, including electrostatic attraction, surface complexation, and ion exchange, have been proposed to explain the interactions between MOFs and toxic/radioactive metal ions. Various traditional and advanced techniques have been applied to analyse the species and microstructures of metal ions adsorbed onto the surface of MOFs at the molecular or atomic level, namely batch experiments (such as kinetic and thermodynamic analyses), appropriate spectroscopic analytical tools, and theoretical calculations.
## 2.1 Batch experimental techniques
Batch experimental techniques are one of the most widely used methods, and are known to be extremely convenient and efficient. This approach can provide valuable information about the adsorption equilibrium time,112,133 maximum adsorption capacity,78,89 and selectivity.106,134
2.1.1 Kinetic analysis. Kinetic analyses can give information about the contact time required for adsorption completion. A study by Zhu et al. reported that the capture of $\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ reached $85\%$ after 5 min and ${>}95\%$ at $10\ \mathrm{min}$ by SCU-101, which was much faster than the adsorption onto commercial resins (A532E and A530E).135 Such fast adsorption kinetics might be attributed to its large channels $(\sim7\times9\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ , $\sim11\ \times\ 5\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , and $\sim4\times2.5\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ ), which are beneficial for $\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ diffusion in the pores of SCU-101. The adsorption equilibrium of many MOF-based materials for metal ion adsorption requires only several minutes,77,82,92,136–140 which is superior to many other widely used adsorbents. Pores within MOFs play an important role in the adsorption of metal ions onto MOFs. Larger pores result in faster diffusion of metal ions within MOFs, which in turn leads to shorter times to reach the adsorption equilibrium. In view of economical wastewater treatment plants, fast adsorption kinetics of the adsorbent are of vital importance for practical applications.
Kinetic analyses can also be used to predict the potential rate-controlling steps, including mass transfer and chemical reaction processes. For instance, Audu et al.99 found that the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ by UiO-66 analogues consisted of an initial slow adsorption process followed by a more rapid adsorption process. These findings suggested that the adsorption process was initially governed by the chemisorption of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ to the readily accessible binding sites near the surfaces of the nanocrystals and became diffusion-limited over time as As(v) anions migrated into the MOF nanoparticles. Generally, adsorption reactions between MOFs and toxic/radioactive metal ions proceed rapidly at the initial stages and gradually slow down upon reaching the adsorption equilibrium. The time required for adsorption completion is dependent on the solution conditions, initial concentration of metal ions and the characteristics of the metal ions and MOFs.
To explore the adsorption kinetics precisely, numerous kinetic models have been applied to simulate the adsorption data, including the pseudo-first-order (PFO) model, pseudo-secondorder (PSO) model, intraparticle diffusion model, Lagergren, Zeldowitsch and Elovich kinetic models.3,5,141 A study by Neaimi et al. applied four kinetic models including PFO, PSO, Elovich, and intraparticle diffusion models to simulate the experimental data of ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ adsorption onto MOF/KNiFC.117 On the basis of the correlation coefficients and calculated $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ values, the PSO model was found to be more appropriate for simulating the adsorption kinetic data, suggesting that the adsorption process was mainly controlled by chemical reactions between ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ and adsorption sites of MOF/KNiFC.117 Generally, the adsorption behaviour of most metal ions onto MOF-based materials follows the PSO model,90,100,104,106,109,112,117,134,136,138,139,142–165 suggesting that chemical adsorption is dominant rather than physical adsorption. Notably, both PFO and PSO models can well describe the overall rate of $\mathrm{As}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ and $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ adsorption onto ZIF-8 nanoparticles. However, both models provide little information about whether external or internal diffusion is the dominant rate limiting step during the adsorption process.166 We also note that $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\mathrm{O}_{7}}^{2-}$ adsorption onto non-protonated MOR-1 and MOR-1-HA composites could be fitted well to the Lagergren’s first order equation.82,92 However, mechanisms of these behaviours remain unclear.
2.1.2 Thermodynamic analysis. Adsorption isotherms under specific solution conditions can provide valuable information for evaluating the mobility or retention of metal ions from the aquatic environments to the solid-phase.167 The adsorption mechanisms, surface properties and the affinity degree of adsorbents can be obtained from physicochemical parameters and underlying thermodynamic assumptions.168 Numerous adsorption isotherm models, such as Langmuir, Freundlich, Brunauer–Emmett–Teller,
Redlich–Peterson, Dubinin–Radushkevich (D–R), Temkin, Toth, Koble–Corrigan, Sips, Flory–Huggins and Radke–Prausnitz isotherm models, have been used to simulate the adsorption isotherms.169 Generally, the behaviours of metal ion adsorption onto MOF-based materials are fitted well by the Langmuir model,82,90,92,100,104–106,109,110,112,117,133,134,136,138,139,142–145,148–150,152, 155,157–161,163,170–177 suggesting that the adsorption behaviour is monolayer-type. Yang et al.116 used Langmuir, Freundlich and D–R models to simulate the isotherm data of U(VI) adsorption onto UiO-66 and GO-COOH/UiO-66 composites. They found that the equilibrium adsorption capacities of these adsorbents increased rapidly in the low concentration range, then increased slowly and finally achieved a maximum equilibrium adsorption capacity. The Langmuir model was more suitable than the Freundlich model for describing the adsorption process, indicating that the adsorption of U(VI) onto these adsorbents was dependent on the completion of the chemisorption monolayer. Furthermore, the $E$ value $(15.38\mathrm{~kJ~mol~}^{-1})$ calculated from the $\mathbf{D-R}$ model was higher than $8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , suggesting that chemical adsorption played the main role in the adsorption process of U(VI) onto the GO-COOH/UiO-66 composites.116
In addition to the adsorption capacity and adsorption mechanism, thermodynamic parameters including the Gibbs free energy change $(\Delta G)$ , enthalpy change $(\Delta H)$ and entropy change $(\Delta S)$ can also be obtained from the temperaturedependent adsorption isotherms. These parameters can further provide insight into the interaction behaviours between metal ions and MOF-based materials. Table 1 lists the reported thermodynamic parameters for metal ion adsorption onto MOF-based materials at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ For example, Zhu et al. found that the $\Delta G$ value of Fe-BTC for As(v) adsorption $(-28\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ was very close to that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ $(-27\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ,150 which demonstrated that the effective adsorption sites in both Fe-BTC and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ were the iron oxide clusters. The $\Delta G$ values of Fe-BTC were less than the values of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , indicating that the effective sites in Fe-BTC were more active. As shown in Table 1, the negative $\Delta G$ and $\Delta S$ values, and positive $\Delta H$ values of the toxic/radioactive metal ion adsorption onto MOF-based materials suggested a spontaneous endothermic process and an increase of entropy after the adsorption. The thermodynamic parameters for adsorption of different metal ions onto different MOF-based materials are quite different, and are dominated by the solution conditions, the properties of the metal ions and the MOF-based materials, and the experimental conditions.
## 2.2 Spectroscopic techniques
Spectroscopic approaches, such as X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy,145,179,180 X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)152 and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy,153 can directly provide the information on adsorption species and microstructures at the molecular scale, which is crucial for analysis of toxic/radioactive metal ion species on the surface of MOFs. These spectroscopic techniques are becoming more and more attractive with the growing studies on wastewater decontamination.6,12,13,32,34,35,181–183
2.2.1 Single technique analysis. XPS analysis can provide the element oxidation states, element species and bonding relationships for all surface and near-surface elements (except for H).184 To date, many studies have used XPS techniques to characterize the adsorption properties of MOF-based materials for toxic/radioactive metal ions.82,92,100,104,105,112,148,150,152,157,162,164,166,170,175,185–188 Researchers in our group used XPS to investigate the interaction mechanism between Sb(III)/Sb(v) oxyanions and NU-1000.107 After the adsorption of $\operatorname{Sb}(\operatorname{III})$ and Sb(v) oxyanions, the ratio of $\mathbf{O_{ad}/O_{l a t t}}$ increased from 1.34 to 1.55 and 1.65, which was mainly attributed to the fact that $\operatorname{sb}(\mathbf{v})$ is associated with six –OH groups, while $\operatorname{Sb}(\operatorname{III})$ is bonded with three –OH groups. The binding energy of the $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{O}}$ site shifted to lower values after Sb adsorption, which indicated that the $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{O}}$ sites played an important role in the Sb adsorption. XPS analysis of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ capture by ZIF-67 revealed the occurrence of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ reduction to $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ during the capture process.152 This phenomenon can be demonstrated by an upward shift of the binding energy of N 1s corresponding to the imidazole groups in ZIF-67 after the uptake of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ onto ZIF-67. XPS results also suggested that the $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ removal mechanism could be attributed to the electrostatic adsorption of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ anions by positively charged ZIF-67 and ion-exchange between the surface hydroxyl groups and $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ . XPS analysis is quantitative and has high surface sensitivity, which permits the detection of most surface-concentrated elements within the top 3–30 atomic layers $\left(10{-}100{\mathring\mathrm{A}}\right)$ in solid samples. Hence, XPS is a powerful tool for characterizing the adsorption reactions between toxic/radioactive metal ions and MOF-based materials. The disadvantage of XPS analysis is that the measurements are performed in a vacuum, which might affect the sample properties.
XAFS including X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectroscopy and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy are well established powerful tools in microstructure characterization. Results obtained from XAFS analysis, such as the type of near-neighbours surrounding targeted ions, coordination number and the bond distances between atoms, can give detailed information about the main adsorption species and the interaction mechanisms.113,116,145,179,189–191 For example, Zhang et al. used XAFS techniques to investigate the local coordination environments around the U(VI) ions in MIL101(Cr) with different ethylenediamine (ED) functionalities before and after U(VI) adsorption.179 As shown in Fig. 3a, similar XANES patterns consisting of a strong main peak (I) and a shoulder II can be seen in all U(VI)-loaded MIL-101(Cr) samples, disclosing the bipyramid skeletal structure of uranyl. The increase of the ED ratio led to changes in the areas of peaks $\mathrm{II/III}$ . The energy positions of the peaks II and III depend on the $\mathbf{U}{-}\mathbf{O}_{\mathrm{ax}}$ and $\mathrm{U-O_{eq}}$ bond distances. Peaks in Fig. 3b correspond to the single scattering paths of oxygen (axial) and the ligand (equatorial). The numbers of axial and equatorial coordinated atoms were two and six, respectively. The authors found that U(VI) was mainly coordinated to the amine groups in ED-MIL-101(Cr), while physical adsorption was dominant in pristine MIL-101(Cr).179 The key advantage of XAFS is that it is element specific and always detectable. The application of XAFS tools will be largely carried out for understanding the speciation and microstructures of toxic/ radioactive metal ions adsorbed onto the surfaces of MOF-based materials with the increasing demand for molecular-scale metal ion-speciation information and the increasing sophistication of the XAFS tools.
In addition, many researchers have performed molecularscale studies to combine their macroscopic adsorption data on metal ion interactions with MOF-based materials using FTIR techniques, owing to its sensitivity to functional groups and highly polar bonds.87,88,112,139,145,148,149,162,164,177,186,192 For example, Ding et al. observed that $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ could replace ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ in MONT-1 by using the FTIR technique.153 The possible formation of the Zr–O–As bonds was evidenced by FTIR spectroscopy after the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ onto UiO-66.177 Strong interactions between $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ species and acylamide or hydroxyl functional groups in $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(hip)(L)]\cdot(DMF)(H_{2}O)}}$ have been observed with the use of FTIR.106 The key advantage of FTIR is that it can be used at concentrations in the micromolar range (optical density $\geq10^{-5}$ ); however, FTIR analysis tends to be qualitative rather than quantitative.
2.2.2 Combined multi-technique analysis. Each spectroscopic technique has its unique advantages, but none is all-powerful. To obtain complete characterization of the adsorption behaviours, results of various surface spectroscopic techniques are usually combined to determine the actual adsorption mechanism. The reliability of the speciation determination and adsorption mechanism generally increases with the number of independent techniques used. Alternatively, FTIR and XPS techniques have been widely combined to confirm the occurrence of adsorption and probe underlying adsorption mechanisms. Jian et al. investigated the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{\mathrm{III}}})$ and As(v) by ZIF-8.166 FTIR and XPS analyses confirmed that the surface Zn hydroxyl, secondary and tertiary amine groups on ZIF-8 served as active sites for arsenic adsorption. The protonated nitrogen-containing groups and $\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{n-OH}$ groups generated by water molecules adsorbed onto ZIF-8 can also adsorb negative As(III) and As(v) species.166 As concluded from FTIR and XPS analysis, the mechanism of roxarsone (ROX) adsorption onto defective UiO-66 involves the bonding interactions between As–OH and reactive $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ groups.100
Bai et al. used FTIR and EXAFS techniques to understand the adsorption mechanism and coordination environment of U(VI) on MIL- ${\cdot101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ , MIL-101-ED $\mathbf{\widehat{ED}}=\mathbf{\widehat{\Pi}}$ ethanediamine) and MIL101-DETA (DETA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ diethylenetriamine).145 FTIR data showed that no ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ counter ions were adsorbed onto MOFs during the U(VI) adsorption, suggesting that U(VI) ions typically appear as hydroxide complexes. EXAFS data disclosed that the obtained $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{U-O_{ax}}}$ bond distance was approximately $1.79\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , indicating that typical uranyl compounds consist of four oxygen atoms and one nitrogen atom in the first equatorial shell. Combined with the FTIR results, it is clear that two hydroxy ions $\left(\mathrm{U}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{eq1}}\right)$ acting as counter ions and coordinating ligands coordinate with $\mathrm{{U}(u)}$ ions at a nearer distance, whereas one amine group $\left(\mathrm{U-N_{\mathrm{eq}}}\right)$ and two water molecules $\left(\mathrm{U}{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{eq}2}\right)$ acting as ligands bind to U(VI) ions at a slightly longer distance.145 Combined with XPS, FTIR and Mossbauer spectroscopy results, Cai et al. proposed that external ${\bf A s O}_{4}^{3-}$ could substitute $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ to coordinate with $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ , and demonstrated the successful capture of ${\bf A s O_{4}}^{3-}$ by MIL-100(Fe).188
To understand the interaction mechanism, nature of the adsorbed species and microstructure at the molecular level, none of the techniques mentioned above can be used alone to characterize the adsorption behaviours completely. Particularly, speciation analysis is extremely difficult for radioactive metal ions because of their low concentration and low crystallinity in MOFs. The combination of multiple techniques can reveal different aspects of the interaction mechanisms of toxic/ radioactive metal ions with MOFs, which is helpful for understanding the adsorption properties of toxic/radioactive metal ions on MOFs.
2.2.3 Other analysis techniques. Other techniques, such as single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and Raman spectroscopy, have also been used to characterize the adsorption behaviours of MOF-based materials.53,77,78,88,157 For example, Fu et al. studied the adsorption of $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}{\mathbf{O}_{7}}^{2-}$ onto FIR-53 with the use of SCXRD and found that $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}{\mathbf{O}_{7}}^{2-}$ anions filled into the channels to balance the framework charge, indicating that ion exchange occurred via single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation.192 It is also observed that $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ anions were fixed into a double layer, and adjoined the edge of the large channel.
## 2.3 Theoretical calculation methods
To obtain an in-depth understanding of the interactions between metal ions and MOF-based materials, theoretical calculations such as density functional theory $\left(\mathrm{DFT}\right)^{98,173}$ and molecular simulation159 are used to provide the structural, force, and total energy information, and the charge density of the system for the adsorption reactions that cannot be given by the current experimental and spectroscopic methods.173,193,194 These results can help researchers to design the structure of MOFs and optimize their adsorption performance.
2.3.1 DFT calculations. DFT is one of the most useful theoretical tools to explain or supplement the experimental results from batch experiments and spectroscopic analysis at the atomic level.14,44,46,195–197
For example, DFT calculations indicate that U(VI) bonding with protonated ligands is more stable than its bonding with deprotonated ligands, which can explain the enhanced adsorption capacity of both MOF-2 and -3 under acidic conditions.171 Moreover, a greater number of protonated groups in MOF-2 results in its higher adsorption capacity than that of MOF-3. According to synchrotron-based powder XRD patterns, Banerjee et al. found that $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ ions were located within the pores of UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ , whereas the O-atom positions of $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ were not confirmed.173 DFT calculations showed that a configuration with three O atoms oriented toward the cluster and one O atom present on a diagonal line away from the cluster (Fig. 4) was the most possible configuration of adsorbed $\mathbf{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ with an adsorption energy of $129.29\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .
DFT calculations can also give information about the most stable adsorption structure, total adsorption energy as well as the system charge density,112,171 which enable identification of underlying mechanisms.46 Yuan et al.198 used the DFT tool to analyse the coordination mode between $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ and the
Schiff base ligands of UIO-66-Schiff base. DFT calculation results revealed that $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ ions coordinated with water to form $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ hydrates $\mathrm{[Co(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+}}$ . The lengths of $_{\mathrm{Co-O_{water}}}$ bonds were approximately 2.169 and $2.162\mathrm{~\AA~},$ which disclosed that $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption onto the UIO-66-Schiff base was an exchange process between the ligands and coordinated $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ hydrates $\mathrm{{[Co(H_{2}O)_{6}]}^{2+}}$ . DFT calculations confirmed that in the structure of $\mathbf{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ incorporated SBN, each $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ coordinated to multiple ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ sites forming strong Ag–O–Re bonds, and to $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine via a hydrogen bond.199 The binding energy difference between SBN and $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ incorporated SBN was $35.61\mathrm{~kcal~mol}^{-1}$ .
Additionally, DFT calculations can describe the adsorption process at the atomic level. For example, Rapti et al. found that $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption on MOR-1-HA could be attributed to the formation of a stable tetrahedral $\mathrm{ArNH}_{2}–\mathrm{CrO}_{3}$ complex, as determined by DFT calculations.92 They proposed that the underlying mechanisms for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption/desorption onto MOR-1-HA involved (i) exchange of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ anions by $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}\mathbf{O_{7}}^{2-}$ anions; (ii) transfer of protons by ${\mathrm{NH_{3}}}^{+}$ groups to $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}{\mathbf{O}_{7}}^{2-}$ anions, resulting in $_{\mathbf{O-Cr}}$ bridging bond rupture, release of a $\mathrm{CrO}_{3}$ fragment, and formation of an ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf C r O_{3}$ complex (reaction: $\mathrm{Ar{-}N H_{3}}^{+}+\mathrm{Cr_{2}O_{7}}^{2-}\rightarrow\mathrm{(Ar{-}N H_{2})C r O_{3}}+\mathrm{HOCrO_{3}}^{-}$ ; and (iii) acid hydrolysis of the ${\bf N H}_{2}–{\bf C r O}_{3}$ unit, release of $\operatorname{cr}(\mathrm{\mathrm{u}})$ species, and regeneration of ${\mathrm{NH}_{3}}^{+}$ functional groups (reaction: $\left[(\mathbf{Ar-NH_{2}})\mathbf{CrO}_{3}\right]+$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{Ar}\mathrm{-NH_{3}}^{+}+\mathrm{HOCrO_{3}}^{-})$ .
To date, several studies have made use of DFT calculations to explain experimental adsorption phenomena.92,112,114,149,159, 171,173,198–200 These approaches can estimate the binding energies, lengths, orientations and charge density of systems for adsorption reactions, which is helpful for achieving a better mechanistic understanding. The disadvantage of this tool is that the energy comparison between the optimized structures remains less accurate.
2.3.2 Molecular simulations. Molecular simulations can provide the positions and potential energies of all adsorbates during the adsorption process. This information is not easily accessible by experimental techniques. Additionally, it can also predict macroscopic phenomena, such as adsorption isotherms and adsorption heats. Over the last decade, molecular simulations have been widely used to predict gas adsorption onto MOFs.201–205 The application of molecular simulations in characterizing the metal ion adsorption on MOF-based materials is still in its infancy.159,206,207
The first study was performed in 2012 by Nalaparaju et al.,207 who conducted a molecular simulation for the exchange of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions with $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions in Na-rho-ZMOF at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ As shown in Fig. 5, initially $\left(t=0\right)$ ), $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ resided in the solution and $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions were in the framework of rho-ZMOF. Upon starting the simulation, $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions rapidly moved into the framework, while $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions moved out. The ion exchange between $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions occurred within a few picoseconds. At $t=0.2$ ns, a large number of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions moved into rho-ZMOF, particularly near the solution/rho-ZMOF interface. Meanwhile, $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions moved out and remained in solution. A portion of the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions also moved into the framework during the ion exchange. Once exchanged, $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions prefer to stay in the framework without moving back to solution. At $t=2$ ns, all the $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions were exchanged and resided in the rho-ZMOF framework. In addition, $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions located at the solution/rho-ZMOF interface also moved into rho-ZMOF. This simulation study provided insight into the molecular level phenomena concerning the ion-exchange process in ionic MOFs.
There have been fewer molecular simulation studies reported on wastewater decontamination by MOF-based materials compared with those on gas adsorption, which can be primarily attributed to the long computational times required to simulate dense liquid phases. However, we expect more simulation studies in this area and deeper molecular insight as computational power increases. With the aid of computational simulation, one can understand the interaction processes of metal ions with MOFs at the molecular level, which are difficult to achieve by experimental measurements. Table 2 summarizes the main purpose, advantages and disadvantages of these characterization techniques mentioned above.
## 3. MOF-based materials for radioactive metal ion decontamination
## 3.1 Uranium
Uranium (U(VI)), mainly present as $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ in aqueous solutions, is a highly toxic species released from mining and industrial activities.8,210 The closure of several nuclear facilities has led to a lot of uranium-contaminated sites in many countries.211 Exposure to uranium can lead to increased cancer risk, and/or liver damage. Chronic ingestion can lead to internal irradiation and chemical toxicity. The United States Environmental Protection Agency (U.S. EPA) has set the acceptable limit for uranium levels in drinking water to be no higher than 30 ppb.212
The extraction of uranium is potentially important for human health and nuclear fuel production. Currently, more and more MOF-based materials have shown great promise as adsorbents for the efficient elimination and preconcentration of U(VI) from aqueous solutions. In the following subsections, we described some typical examples of pristine MOFs and modified MOFs with high adsorption efficiency for the elimination of U(VI) ions. Uranium adsorption data for reported MOF-based materials are assessed and compared with those of other adsorbents.
3.1.1 With pristine MOFs. The first example of MOFs applied as novel adsorbents to capture U(VI) from aqueous media was
UiO-68-type MOFs (MOF-2 and -3).171 Maximum adsorption capacities of 217 and $188~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ were found for MOF-2 at $\mathrm{pH}\sim2.5$ in water and simulated seawater, respectively, which were much higher than for MOF-3 (109 and $32\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}.$ ). The authors performed DFT calculations to explain the observed enhanced adsorption capacity of MOF-2, and proposed that this phenomenon was traceable to the possible coordination mode of the phosphorylurea moiety with $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ ions. The structure of $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ ions binding with protonated ligands was more stable than that binding with protonated ones. Fig. 6 shows the optimized representative binding structures of $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ coordinated to one and two protonated ligands. The binding geometry of II–II was most enthalpically favoured with $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ ions coordinated to two phosphoryl oxygen atoms. DFT calculations could further account for the enhanced adsorption capacity in acidic solutions. The authors also found that the adsorbed amounts of $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ ions in MOF-2 and -3 were not notably affected by treatment with 0.01 M HCl, while approximately $31\mathrm{-}66\%$ was eluted by treating with $_{0.1\textbf{M}}$ HCl. Notably, some U(VI) ions remained in MOF-2 and -3, even after treatment with 1 M HCl. However, the selectivity and reusability of MOF-2 and -3 require further investigation.171 Shortly afterward, another luminescent MOF (MOF-76) was reported by Shi’s group, which showed a higher adsorption capacity $(298\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ towards U(VI) at solution $\mathrm{pH}\ 2.5\mathrm{-}3$ , while its capacity decreased considerably at solution $\mathrm{\pH}\sim5$ (less than $100\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}\cdot$ ) and in the presence of various interfering metal cations $\left(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{s}^{+}\right.$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Sr}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ ) ( $\cdot{\sim}110\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .133 The authors proposed that the adsorbed U(VI) ions were retained in the pores of MOF-76 because the pore size was large enough to trap the U(VI) species with a linear structure.133 However, it is not clear why MOF-76 had such an unusually high adsorption capacity in acidic solution. Unlike other established MOF adsorbents that functioned in acidic solutions, Co-SLUG-35 is applicable in alkaline solutions and showed an adsorption capacity of $118~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .157 The complete replacement of $\mathbf{EDS}^{2-}$ anions by U(VI) ions via ion exchange was evidenced by FTIR, XRD and XPS analysis. This adsorption mechanism resulted in the superior selectivity for U(VI) ions. Additionally, this MOF also showed excellent performance in U(VI) extraction from seawater. This work presents an extremely rare example of an adsorbent with effectiveness for U(VI) capture in alkaline solutions.
3.1.2 With modified MOFs. To date, many studies have confirmed that functionalization of pristine MOFs is an effective way to enhance the adsorption performances of MOFs. A study from Zhang et al. investigated coumarin-loaded MOF-74 for $\mathrm{\DeltaU(v)}$ adsorption.158 The authors prepared MOF-74 through a solvothermal method (A1) and at room temperature (A2), respectively. The A2 MOF showed high U(VI) adsorption capacity $(\sim300\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ ) compared with that of A1 MOF $(200\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}})$ . This phenomenon could be explained by the difference of the pore size between these two MOFs. The A2 MOF was both microporous and mesoporous, whereas the A1 MOF was microporous with a narrow pore size distribution (7.9 Å). The larger size pores of the A2 MOF promoted the diffusion of U(VI) ions into the MOF framework. Another novel feature of this work was that the introduction of coumarin molecules into A2 enhanced its adsorption capacity to a certain extent, because coumarin provided additional adsorption sites for binding of U(VI) ions. Furthermore, up to $80\%$ of $\mathrm{U}(\mathrm{v})$ could be desorbed from the MOF-74 through treatment with $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{~M~HCl}}$ or ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ . One disadvantage of MOF-74 was its narrow active pH range for U(VI) adsorption.
Uranyl cations can be regarded as a strong Lewis acid with a high affinity for hard oxygen donors including hydroxide and carboxylate,213 thus the introduction of carboxyl units into MOFs can considerably enhance their adsorption efficiency.214 Recently, a novel carboxyl-functionalized MIL-101 was applied to U(VI) adsorption.159 The saturation adsorption capacity of this MOF was as high as $314~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was much higher than that of pristine MIL-101 and amino-functionalized MIL-101.145 This difference was attributed to the large amount of available carboxyl groups in this MOF. Excellent selectivity over a range of interfering metal cations, particularly $\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and $\operatorname{Sr}^{2+}$ , was observed due to the better affinity of carboxyl groups towards U(VI) ions. Multinuclear hydroxide species of $\mathbf{U}(\mathrm{v})$ are more favoured under those solution conditions. Two kinds of coordination modes were suggested by DFT calculations. Specifically, one mode involved carbonyl oxygen coordinated to the central uranium in a monodentate fashion with four water molecules in the first solvation shell. This representative coordination mode can further explain the excellent affinity and selectivity of this MOF in the extraction of U(VI) from complicated aqueous solutions.
3.1.3 With MOF composites. MOFs combined with other functional materials can achieve considerably enhanced adsorption performance compared with that of individual counterparts through synergistic effects among components with rationally designed properties. A recent study by Yang et al. concerned the design of GO-COOH/UiO-66 composites for U(VI) extraction from simulated seawater.116 The authors found that this composite maintained efficient adsorption in solution of $\mathsf{p H}6{\-}9$ , owing to the introduction of GO-COOH sheets. The adsorption capacity of this composite was improved up to $188.3~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ because of the additional adsorption sites offered by the GO-COOH sheets. Analysis of the FTIR and XPS results disclosed that U(VI) adsorption onto this composite occurred through chelation and ion-exchange. This composite also featured better selectivity and reusability. However, reducing the cost and scaling up production of GO-COOH/UiO-66 are still challenges for applications in real seawater.
Separation of U(VI) ions from lanthanides is also a great challenge in nuclear waste reprocessing because of their chemical similarities. To this end, magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ showed remarkable selectivity towards ${\mathrm{U}}({\mathrm{v}})/{\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}$ at $\mathsf{p H}=3$ .115 This composite displayed an ultrahigh adsorption capacity of $523.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was considerably higher than that of many reported MOF-based adsorbents. The authors suggested that the main factors contributing to the adsorption of U(VI) onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\textcircled{\mathrm{a}}\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ were the coordination between Zn atoms and U(VI) ions together with the hydrogen bonding. The magnetic properties of this material led to rapid and easy separation from solutions. Despite the excellent adsorption selectivity and the facile separation, application of this composite is currently limited to laboratory. The realization of the fast and simple separation of U(VI) based on magnetic MOF composites is still a great challenge.
3.1.4 Comparison between MOF-based materials and with other adsorbents. In addition to the above-mentioned MOF-based materials, many other MOF-based materials have been reported for U(VI) adsorption, which are tabulated in Table 3. Ion exchange is the main mechanism of U(VI) adsorption onto pristine MOFs owing to their highly-ordered structures.133,145,157,158,160,171,206 For modified MOFs and MOF composites, coordination with functional groups is an alternative interaction mechanism.115,159,179,215–217 The highest adsorption capacity for U(VI) has been reported to be $787.4~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for HKUST;161 however, the most selective MOF-based adsorbent for U(VI) reported to date is $\mathbf{GO-COOH}/\$ UiO-66,116 which exhibited high adsorption efficiency in the presence of metal ions with various valence states. Importantly, unlike other MOFs where degassing was primarily needed, Co-SLUG-35 could be used without any pre-treatments.157 Detailed U(VI) adsorption study has been performed for MIL101-DETA,145 $\mathrm{GO-COOH/UiO-}66^{116}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}^{-8}$ ,115 which showed relatively fast adsorption kinetics and high selectivity, but with limited reusability. However, the active $\mathsf{p H}$ range of MIL-101-DETA was very narrow $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 4.5\mathrm{-}5.5\right)$ . Note that no studies have been performed to evaluate the radiation stability of these MOFs for further assessment of their feasibility in practical application, which is a crucial aspect for applications in the radioactive waste management, especially after high preconcentration of radioactive $\mathbf{U}(\mathrm{v})$ ions.
It is also useful to compare U(VI) adsorption properties of MOFbased materials with other widely used adsorbents. As shown in Table 3, several MOF-based materials have shown superior adsorption capacities (up to ${\sf787.4~m g~g^{-1}}.$ ),115,133,145,158,159,161 which were much higher than those of other widely used adsorbents, such as AC (only a few dozen ${\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}}.$ ),218 carbonaceous materials $\left(125\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ ,14 clay minerals (only a few dozen ${\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}},$ ,219 metal oxides $\left(<100~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ),220,221 LDHs $\left(26{-}330\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ,222,223 NZVI $(215\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}};$ ),23,224 GOs $(100{-}299~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ 116,225,226 and CNTs $\left(19{-}212\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right).$ ),227,228 and approximated to those of metal sulfide ion exchangers (MSIEs) $380\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for KMS-1),36 chalcogenides $\left(338\mathrm{~\textit~{~m~g~}~}\mathrm{\boldmath~g~}^{-1}\right)^{229}$ and mesoporous silica (409).230–233 The adsorption kinetics and recyclability of AC, clay minerals, carbonaceous materials and LDHs tend to be poorer and slower, compared with those of MOFs.161,171,234 Metal oxides, LDH and mesoporous silica are unstable under extreme alkaline or acidic conditions (owing to immediate dissolution of aluminium/silicon ions) of nuclear waste.235 NZVI showed low selectivity, narrow working $\mathsf{p H}$ range, and rapid deactivation.236,237 CNTs showed low selectivity for U(VI) owing to the lack of specific functional groups. The inexpensive hydrothermal synthesis of MOFs is much more attractive than the multistep preparation of GOs which requires high cost organic reagents and solvents.238 Comparing MOFs with MSIEs, many MSIEs have low regeneration capabilities and reusability after saturation with U(VI) ions, whereas some MOFs show excellent reusability after several cycles.115,145,159
Currently, the design of seawater applicable adsorbents is more promising for the selective extraction of radioactive U(VI) from seawater. UiO-68-type MOFs,171,217 Co-SLUG-35,157 $\mathrm{GO-COOH/UiO-66}^{116}$ have been reported to be effective in simulated seawater, representing promising alternatives for U(VI) extraction. Additionally, several MOF-based adsorbents115,145,159,160 have shown excellent selectivity for U(VI) with interfere from lanthanides such as ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{sm}^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{3+}$ , $\boldsymbol{\Upsilon}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{b}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ . Efficient separation of actinides from lanthanides remains a difficult problem in nuclear waste management and is a crucial aspect in spent fuel reprocessing. MOF-based composites could contribute to the selective extraction of actinides from lanthanides if proper structures are constructed and surface functional groups are introduced into MOF-based composites.
## 3.2 Other radioactive metal ions
Other radioactive nuclides, such as $\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Th}^{4+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ and $\operatorname{Sr}^{2+}$ , are highly toxic and hazardous to human health and the environment.47 In recent years, MOF-based materials have shown great potential in the enrichment of radioactive metal ions from radioactive wastewater.53,88,112,239,240 In the following section, we summarize some typical examples of radioactive metal ion adsorption onto MOF-based adsorbents and compare their adsorption performances with those of other widely used adsorbents.
3.2.1 With pristine MOFs. To date, several MOFs have been applied as potential $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}/\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ adsorbents. Fei et al. reported the adsorption capacity of SLUG-21 for $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ to be $602\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ,88 which overcame the bottleneck of low adsorption capacity $(125~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and retention problems of hydrotalcites. SLUG-21 also exhibited other advantageous properties including complete heterogeneity and no need for pre-treatments. Recently, Wang’s group reported a 3D cationic MOF SCU-100 with a remarkable feature of excellent $\upbeta-$ and $\gamma$ -radiation-resistance.53 As shown in Fig. 7a, SCU-100 displayed faster adsorption kinetics for $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ than commercial A532E and A530E owing to its ordered 1D channels facilitating the diffusion of $\mathbf{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ anions. The adsorption capacity $(541\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ was much higher than those of Mg–Al LDH, NDTB-1, $\mathbf{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{5}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ and $\mathrm{Yb}_{3}\mathrm{O(OH)}_{6}\mathrm{Cl}$ (Fig. 7b), and the distribution coefficient $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ reached $1.9\ \times$ $10^{5}~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . By solving the single crystal structure of SCU-100- Re, the authors found that the adsorbed $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}$ anions were trapped in the void spaces of the 1D channels in the SCU-100 (Fig. 7c). Each $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ anion was coordinated by many hydrogen bonds and two ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ cations forming $\mathbf{Ag-O-Re}$ bonds (Fig. 7d). The relatively soft ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ sites preferentially bound to the relatively soft anions with lower charge densities, which led to the excellent selectivity of SCU-100 for $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ over ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ . One clear drawback of SCU-100 is that large single crystals disintegrated into microcrystalline materials after anion exchange. Additionally, the SCU-100 was prepared from precious ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and the preparation process was complicated. Thus, there is a need to prepare SCU100 on a large scale at low cost by a simple method for practical applications.
3.2.2 With modified MOFs. MIL-101 functionalized with sulfate and sulfonic acid groups was studied for the capture of radioactive $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ ions.112 The adsorption kinetics of $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{a}^{2+}$ ions onto both modified MIL-101 were fitted well to the PSO kinetic model with high $k_{2}$ values. Based on the Langmuir model, the adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{MOF{-}808{-}S O_{4}}$ $\mathrm{131~mg~g^{-1}},$ ) was higher than that of $\mathbf{MIL}–101–\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{Cr})$ $\left(70.05\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right).$ . One reasonable explanation for this result is that the sulfate groups grafted onto the metal cluster were more accessible than sulfonic groups introduced into the ligands. The results of FTIR, XPS and DFT analysis indicated a stronger binding interaction between the sulfate groups and $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ ions, which could explain the higher selectivity of MOF- $808\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ than that of M $\mathrm{IL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}(\mathrm{Cr})$ . Additionally, the adsorption of $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{a}^{2+}$ ions by these MOFs was irreversible, which avoided secondary pollution. It is not yet known whether the frameworks of these MOFs are damaged by the effects of radiation. This is a crucial requirement for realworld applications under the radiation conditions.
Recently, a study from Shi’s group found that the introduction of carboxyl groups into UiO-66 (UiO-66-COOH and UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOH)}_{2}$ could enhance its adsorption ability towards $\mathrm{Th}(\mathrm{rv})$ 239 As shown in Fig. 8a, the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Th}(\mathrm{rv})$ onto UiO-66- $\left(\mathrm{COOH}\right)_{2}$ was as high as $60\mathrm{mgg}^{-1}$ even at $\mathrm{pH}2.0$ , whereas UiO-66 showed negligible adsorption efficiency. This phenomenon highlighted the important role of carboxyl groups in the efficient binding of $\mathrm{Th}(\mathrm{rv})$ ions. For practical applications, the acid resistance of adsorbents should also be taken into consideration. The combination of electrostatic interactions and binding effects of carboxyl groups promoted the rapid adsorption kinetics of UiO-66- $\left(\mathrm{COOH}\right)_{2}$ (Fig. 8b), which fit well to the PSO model, suggesting the chemical adsorption process. Additionally, UiO-66- $\left(\mathrm{COOH}\right)_{2}$ displayed a saturated adsorption capacity of $350~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathrm{pH}=3.0$ , which represented a milestone in $\mathrm{{Th}(\mathrm{{rv})}}$ capture by solid extraction. The possible adsorption mechanism evidenced by the FTIR and EXAFS analysis was the coordination reaction between $\mathrm{Th}(\mathrm{rv})$ ions and carboxyl groups, which led to the excellent adsorption preference for Th(IV) over a range of competing metal ions.239 However, partial damage of the framework was observed after $\mathrm{{Th}(\mathrm{{r})}}$ desorption from acid solutions.
3.2.3 Comparison between MOF-based materials and other adsorbents. Table 4 summarizes radioactive metal ion adsorption characteristics of MOF-based materials that have been reported in the literature. The radioactive metal ion adsorption properties of MOF-based materials have been investigated for relatively few radioactive metal ions. No studies on the extraction of other radioactive metal ions such as $\mathrm{Am(m),Np(w)},$ and $\mathrm{Pu(w)}$ by MOF-based materials have been reported. Few studies of radioactive metal ion adsorption onto MOF-based materials have been performed in detail. Furthermore, the extent of radiation damage to the frameworks of most MOFs remains unclear.
The highest adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{ReO_{4}}^{-}/\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ was found to be $786~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for SBN;199 however, the most selective MOF was SCU-101,135 which could selectively capture $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ in the presence of competing anions (e.g. ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-`}.$ ) $\left(K_{\mathrm{d}}~\ge~7.5~\times~10^{5}~\mathrm{mL~g}^{-1}\right)$ . The adsorption performances of SBN, $^{199}\mathrm{SLUG}{-21}^{241}$ and SCU-10053 were superior to those of anion exchange resins $(252\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}),$ 242 GO-based materials $\left(<44~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ,243 NZVI $\left(28.73\mathrm{\mg\y^{-1}}\right)$ ,31 and other cost-effective adsorbents such as LDHs.244–247 Anion exchange resins might lose capacity when exposed to highly alkaline solutions or high radiation doses.248 Owing to the noncomplexing nature, high water solubility and great stability of $\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ , considerable work remains to improve the adsorbent capacity, kinetics, and selectivity.
UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ showed a saturation capacity of $167~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions,240 which was much higher than those of macrocyclic ligand functionalized silica $\left(83\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}\right).$ ),249 modified chitosan $(<138~\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ,250–252 and comparable to that of calixcrown modified micro-sized silica $(160\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}})$ .253 Pd(II) recovery often involves treatment by strong ${\bf H N O}_{3}$ . $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ shows more promise for convenient recovery of the $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ from extremely acidic high-level liquid wastes owing to its high adsorption capacity, easy synthesis and strong acid-resistance. The $\mathrm{\mathbf{Ba}(\mathrm{II})}$ uptake capacity of $\mathbf{MOF{-}808–S O_{4}}$ outperformed a wide range of adsorbents, such as AC,254 clays,255 and titanate nanotubes.256 Although titanate nanofibers showed a higher adsorption capacity than $\mathbf{MIL}–101–\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H(Cr)},$ the $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}(\mathbf{Cr})$ showed additional promising features for applications, such as ultrafast adsorption kinetics.
The highest adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{Th}^{4+}$ was shown by UiO-66- $\left(\mathrm{COOH}\right)_{2}$ $(350\mathrm{~mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , and this was considerably larger than those reported for other adsorbents, such as CNTs $(13.22\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,257 chrysotile nanotubes $(40.39\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,258 impregnated resin $\left(198.5\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right)^{259}$ and GO $(<214\ \mathrm{\mg\~g^{-1}})$ .47,260 Recently, thorium has drawn interest as a most potential alternative to uranium as a fertile material for producing nuclear fuel.239 Additionally, thorium and uranium often occur together in minerals.261 Thus the separation of thorium from uranium requires further investigation.
A MOF-based adsorbent with the highest ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ adsorption capacity was a 3D uranyl organic framework $(432\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ ,96 representing one of the largest values for ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ capture by solid extraction. Other reported adsorbents showed relatively low adsorption capacities, for example, o20 mg g1 for clay minerals,262,263 $<185~\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for GO-based materials13 and $280\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ for MSIE (KMS-2).36,271 Metal oxides such as manganese oxides showed low selectivity for ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ in the presence of high salt concentrations.272 It is important to highlight that the 3D uranyl organic framework displayed high b- and g-radiation-resistance.96 MOF-based adsorbents studied for adsorption of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{m})^{97,267}$ and $\mathrm{Eu(m)^{144}}$ showed relatively low adsorption capacities, which indicates a better scope for developing advanced MOFs for the capture of $\mathrm{{Co}(\mathrm{{II})/\mathrm{{Eu}(\mathrm{{III})}}}}$ and/or other radioactive metal ions (e.g. $\mathrm{Sr(II)}$ , $\mathrm{Am}\mathrm{(m)}$ , $\mathrm{{Np}(\mathrm{{r})}}$ and $\mathrm{Pu(w)})$ from nuclear waste.
## 4 MOF-based materials for heavy metal decontamination
## 4.1 Chromium ions
Chromium ions and their corresponding compounds are carcinogens released into the environment in the form of oxyanions $\left(\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}\right.$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ or $\mathbf{HCrO_{4}}^{-\cdot}$ and cations $(\mathbf{Cr^{3+}})$ from various industrial processes, such as leather tanning, cement production, and metal plating.273–275 In the following subsections, we summarize and assess different kinds of MOFs (3D MOFs, layered MOFs, 1D MOFs and MOF composites) with high adsorption efficiency for the efficient elimination of chromium ions.
4.1.1 With 3D MOFs. The first 3D cationic MOF studied for $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ adsorption was the heterometallic 3D framework $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}}$ , which has strong alkali resistance.276 $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}}$ was able to remove $\sim70\%$ of $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ (initially at $\sim580\mathrm{\ppm}_{,\ }$ within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ and $85\%$ of $\mathbf{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ at equilibrium (after $\sim6\mathrm{~h~}$ ) via a mechanism of ion exchange with $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ . The adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}}$ reached $\sim63~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In addition, $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}}$ was selective for $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ from a solution containing $\nabla{\bf O}_{3}^{-},\mathrm{Cl}^{-},\mathrm{Br}^{-},\mathrm{I}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ . The mechanisms of selectivity and reusability require further study. Recently, a cationic silver-triazolate MOF $\left(1{\cdot}\mathbf{NO}_{3}\right)^{175}$ which could be synthesized on a gram-scale displayed fast and reversible adsorption of $\mathbf{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ via anionexchange showing a moderate adsorption capacity $\left(37.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ . $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}$ showed different selectivity performances for $\mathbf{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ over common interfering anions such as ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ with different concentrations.175 Hofmeister bias played a crucial role in this selectivity performance. Compared with other adsorbents for $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ , the higher cost and lower capacity of $\mathbf{\left.1-NO_{3}\right.}$ might hinder its practical application in chromium removal.
For another kind of chromium oxyanions $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{Cr}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{7}^{2-}\right)$ , several 3D MOFs have been applied for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption. $^{87,192,277,278}$ Among these studies, $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption onto 3D MOFs via anion-exchanged single-crystal-to-single-crystal (SC–SC) transformation is notable. The first case involved 3D cationic ABT $\mathrm{2ClO_{4}}$ , which showed a high adsorption capacity of $213{-}271\ \mathrm{mg\y^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ through SC–SC ion exchange.277 The $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ exchange occurred slowly as observed by UV-Vis spectroscopy and required $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ to reach equilibrium. The exact position of the trapped $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ could not be determined exactly from SCXRD data owing to severe disorder. Selectivity studies have revealed that ABT $2\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ preferentially adsorbed $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ over ${\mathbf{NO}_{3}}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ owing to the stronger affinity of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}^{2-}$ for the cationic framework than other interfering anions. Li et al. recently reported a new 3D MOF showing a relatively high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ $(207\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\nu i a$ ion-exchange in a SC–SC fashion.278 The $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ ions were adsorbed into the 1D channels by the formation of multiple weak $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}$ interactions with the host framework. Additionally, this MOF showed excellent selectivity and the adsorption efficiency remained high in the presence of various anions (e.g. $\mathbf{F}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{\oAc^{-}}$ , ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B{F_{4}}^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-}$ and $\mathbf{Cr}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{7}{}^{2-})$ . However, the selectivity and reusability remain unexplained; the chemical stability of this MOF should be improved because its single-crystallinity was destroyed after the release of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ ions in recycling processes.
4.1.2 With layered MOFs. Two layered MOFs (FIR- $53^{192}$ and $\mathbf{Ag}–3^{279})$ have been reported for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption through ion exchange. As shown in Fig. 9a, FIR-53 showed a $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption capacity of $\sim74\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ with very fast adsorption kinetics and only 10 min was required to achieve equilibrium at a low initial $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ concentration $\left(2\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}}\right)$ .192
Competitive adsorption results revealed that FIR-53 showed a high selectivity for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ over $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ and ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ . Importantly, the regenerated FIR-53 retained its initial adsorption capacity even after five cycles (Fig. 9b). The SC–SC transformation proceeded during $\mathrm{{Cr}}_{2}{\bf{O}}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption as evidenced by the SCXRD data, which showed that the adsorbed $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ anions were fixed into the double layers of FIR-53 through the formation of weak $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}$ interactions (Fig. 9c).
4.1.4 With MOF composites. Recently, researchers in our group synthesized a kind of well-defined core–double-shell structured magnetic polydopamine@ZIF-8 $\left(\mathbf{MP}@\mathbf{ZIF-}8\right)$ hybrids through a facile and green approach (Fig. 10a), which displayed an uptake capacity of $136.56\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{{Cr}_{2}{O_{7}}^{2-}}$ via synergistic reduction and adsorption mechanisms (Fig. 10b).282 The adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ by this composite followed the PSO model, indicating a chemical reaction process. Additionally, the uptake of $\mathrm{{Cr}}_{2}{\bf{O}}_{7}{}^{2-}$ by $\mathbf{MP}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}{-}8$ was highly dependent on solution pH and the capacity remained $42.59\mathrm{~mg}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ at pH 9.05. XPS results revealed that owing to the reductive properties of the nitrogen-containing groups on ZIF-8 and PDA, $\mathrm{{Cr}(v)}$ was easily converted into $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ with a lower toxicity, which was then immobilized on $\mathbf{MP}@\mathbf{ZIF}–8$ (Fig. 10c). Optimization of MOF composites for metal ion uptake and the preparation of new MOF-based adsorbents are currently underway in our group.
Rapti et al. found that MOFs combined with alginic acid (MOR-1-HA82,92 and MOR-2-HA140) were excellent adsorbents for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ ions and showed high adsorption capacities of
$162{-}280\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ via a mechanism of ion exchange. MOR-2 showed high efficiency over a wide $\mathrm{pH}$ range (2–9) and superior selectivity for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ over common interfering anions such as $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , and ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ . Such high selectivity has also been observed in potable and industrial water systems. The adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ by MOR-2 was completed within 1 min. The combination of theoretical calculations and experimental results showed that the excellent adsorption ability of MOR-2 was determined by the bulky pyridyl-methyl-ammonium functional groups. In the structure of $\mathbf{MOR{-}}2(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{Cr}_{2}\mathbf{O_{7}}^{2-}$ and MOR$2\textcircled{\bf{\hat{a}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{rO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ or $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ ions were coordinated to the $\mathrm{zr}_{6}$ clusters which acted as ligands in a $\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{1}{:}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{1}{:}\ensuremath{\upmu}_{2}$ -fashion.
Except for the batch experimental technique, the adsorption efficiency of these MOF-based composites was also tested with the use of continuous flow columns.82,92,140 As shown in Fig. 11, MOR-2-HA was mixed with silica sand as an inert material to ensure a stable flow rate of the aqueous solutions through the column. Adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ was clearly observed (Fig. 11a). Importantly, the column could be easily regenerated through treatment with 4 M HCl solution and retained high adsorption capacity after five cycles of the column. Eleven bed volumes of the effluent samples showed a total Cr content $\leq10$ ppb (Fig. 11b), which was below the acceptable levels. In addition, the MOR-2-HA/sand column was also effective at treating chrome-plating wastewater samples (Fig. 11c), which indicates its potential for real wastewater decontamination. The MOR-1-HA/sand column also showed excellent adsorption efficiency.82,92 The high adsorption performance of these MOFbased composites indicates promise for applications in the treatment of Cr-contaminated wastewater.
4.1.5 Comparison between MOF-based materials and other adsorbents. Many studies have focused on developing superior
MOF-based materials for chromium adsorption. Table 5 lists the recent reports on chromium adsorption characteristics of MOF-based materials. The main adsorption mechanism was ion exchange. Only five MOFs have been studied for $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ adsorption including $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}}$ ,276 SLUG-21,88 Zn-Co-SLUG-35,280 $\mathbf{1-NO_{3}}^{175}$ and ZIF-67.152 Although $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}$ showed a relatively low adsorption capacity, it could be achieved in a gram-scale synthesis. $\mathbf{^{175}N o}$ information was available about their adsorption capacity in solutions of different pH. ZIF-67 showed high reusability although its adsorption capacity was only $5.88\mathrm{-}13.34~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .152 Many kinds of MOF-based materials have been studied for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption. MOR-1-HA,82,92 MOR-2-HA140 and Fe–gallic acid $\mathbf{MOFS}^{281}$ show good efficiency over a wide pH range. Column chromium adsorption data exist only for the MOR-1-HA and MOR-2-HA composites.82,92,140 High adsorption capacities $\left(>200~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ were achieved for several MOFs,82,92,151,277,278,283 with the highest capacity (up to $1709.2\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}},$ obtained by the Fe-gallic acid MOF.281 The performance of this material is considerably higher than those of others, such as bioadsorbents $(333\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ ,285,286 metal/metal oxide-based materials $\left(4{-}473\mathrm{~mg~g}^{-1}\right).$ ,287,288 LDHs $(90-183~\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ,289–292 organic resins $(110-140~\mathrm{\mg~{\g}^{-1})};$ 42 carbon or GO-based materials $\left(23{-}581\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right).$ ),293,294 and hybrid polymeric materials $\left(90{-}377~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right).$ ).295,296
In addition to the adsorption capacity, it is also necessary to consider the adsorption kinetics, selectivity and regeneration of the chromium adsorption properties of MOFs in comparison with those properties of common adsorbents such as inexpensive
LDHs, metal oxide-based materials and organic resins. Although LDHs are inexpensive, they show slow chromium adsorption kinetics, and their adsorption completion cannot be achieved within several hours.289–291 Note that some MOFs can attain equilibrium within $3~\mathrm{min}$ .82,92,140 In addition, LDHs often show low selectivity for chromium adsorption in the presence of common competitive anions (e.g., ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}\mathcal{I}$ ,289 whereas MOFs usually display a high chromium preference over various other ions (such as halide, ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ ).87,192 The pH range for efficient heavy metal adsorption by metal oxide-based materials is very narrow,297 whereas several MOFs show efficient adsorption over a wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range. $^{82,92,281}$ Organic resins are oxidized by $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{u})}}$ and cannot be regenerated or reused.298 Conversely, several MOFs can be easily regenerated after chromium adsorption and reused several times with only a small loss in their capacity.82,87,92,94,143,152,153,165,192,299 In addition, the preparation of organic resins, GO-based materials and hybrid polymeric materials usually requires multistep reactions, some of which use costly organic reagents and solvents.42 Conversely, many MOFs can be prepared via one-step hydrothermal syntheses.84,175 Compared with the low yield of GO and CNTs, the synthesis of $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}$ is fast, simple and can be performed on a gram-scale.175 In these respects, MOFs show great prospects in $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{v})}}$ remediation applications.
## 4.2 Arsenic ions
Arsenic, produced by the combustion of fossil fuels, mining and metallurgical activities, is a highly toxic heavy metal that exists naturally in groundwater.300 As(v) mainly presents as oxyanion species in aqueous solutions, while the species of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{m}})$ (either neutral or anionic forms) is related to the solution $\mathsf{p H}$ . Note that $\mathrm{As}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ is more toxic and difficult to remove than $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ . In addition, organoarsenic forms can also transform into As(v) and $\mathrm{As}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ species. Long-term exposure to arsenic-polluted water would cause some health problems, such as kidney, lung, liver, bladder, and skin cancers.301–303 The U.S. EPA has mandated the levels of arsenic in drinking water to be less than 10 ppb for safe consumption.304 Recently, MOF-based materials have drawn considerable interest as adsorbents for arsenic removal. In the following subsections, we assess the adsorption characteristics of As(v), As(III), and organoarsenic species by MOFbased materials, respectively.
4.2.1 MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{A}\pmb{s}(\mathbf{v})$ adsorption. The first MOF used as an adsorbent for the adsorption of arsenic species was Fe-BTC which was obtained through a facile solvothermal method.150 The adsorption capacity of Fe-BTC for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ was only $12.3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ; however, this value was still more than 6 times as high as that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles and 36 times higher than that of commercial $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ powders. The observed Fe–O–As groups in the IR spectra of As(v)-loaded Fe-BTC confirmed the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ ions. Interestingly, arsenium elements were not detected by XPS in this study, suggesting that adsorbed As(v) ions were trapped in the interior of the Fe-BTC rather than at the outer surface. A disadvantage of this MOF was its instability under basic conditions. However, this work demonstrates the potential for applications of MOFs to the capture of arsenic species.150 ZIFs, in particular differently structured ZIF-8, have been applied to the adsorption of As(v).148,305 ZIF-8 nanoparticles showed an adsorption capacity of $76.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a low equilibrium concentration of $9.8~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ .148 Microporous ZIF-8 with a hierarchical structure prepared in different solvents and surfactants showed obviously different adsorption capacities.305 Among these ZIFs, hierarchically structured ZIF-8 showed the highest adsorption capacity $\left(90.92~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ , which might be attributed to two possible reasons: (i) mesostructured pores leading to the improved utility of the external surface of ZIF-8 crystals with mesostructured pores; and (ii) enhanced porosity facilitating mass transfer within the pore structure. Further investigations of the active working pH, reusability, and selectivity of these ZIFs were not performed.
The MIL families are another example of MOFs applied to $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ adsorption, such as $\small\mathrm{MIL-5}3\big(\mathrm{Fe}\big),^{142}\ \mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}53\big(\mathrm{Al}\big)^{147}$ and MIL-100(Fe).188 MIL-53(Al) was efficient over a solution pH range of 6–9 with a maximum adsorption capacity of $105.6\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ owing to its strong breathing effects. In addition, the maximum adsorption capacity of MIL-53(Al) was considerably decreased in the presence of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ anions because of their similarity to ${\bf A s O_{4}}^{3-}$ .147 MIL-53(Fe) showed fast $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ adsorption kinetics at three different initial concentrations and required only $60{-}150\ \mathrm{\min}$ to reach equilibrium owing to the strong interaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{AsO}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations and their electrostatic interactions.142 MIL-100(Fe) had a high adsorption capacity of $110\mathrm{\mg\8^{-1}}$ for ${\bf A s O}_{4}^{3-}$ with good reusability and excellent selectivity in the presence of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ , ${\bf N O}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ .188 FTIR studies have indicated that the formation of Fe–O–As(v) bonds could explain the high adsorption performance of MIL-100(Fe). Interestingly, the long-range order of the uniform mesoporous channels of MIL-100(Fe) was damaged by adsorption of ${\bf A s O}_{4}^{3-}$ and recovered immediately upon ${\bf A s O_{4}}^{3-}$ desorption. This study extends our understanding of the longrange order of MOFs and promotes investigations of metal adsorption by MOFs.
Zr-Based MOFs, in particular MOF- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}808^{146}$ and UiO-66,177 have also been used to study the adsorption properties of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ . MOF-808 showed an adsorption capacity of $24.8~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ owing to weak interactions between $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ ions and $\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{{(rv)}}$ metal nodes. The kinetics of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ adsorption onto MOF-808 fitted well to the PSO model and $95\%$ of the initial $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ was captured in only $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ of contact time. Additionally, $82.1\%$ of the adsorption efficiency remained after five regeneration/reuse cycles.146 All the above MOFs showed moderate adsorption capacities for $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ . UiO-66 showed an ultrahigh adsorption capacity of $303.34~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{p H}2$ , which was much higher than those of other synthetic or commercial adsorbents $\left(5{-}280\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ , generally $<100\mathrm{mgg^{-1}}\qquad.$ ). Furthermore, UiO-66 was efficient under a very wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range $\mathrm{(pH~1-10)}$ . Additionally, the adsorption efficiency of UiO-66 was not affected by the presence of other competitive anions ${\left({\bf N O}_{3}{}^{-}\right.}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ ).177 The superior adsorption properties of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ can be traceable to its highly porous structure, large contact area and abundant active sites. The terminal hydroxyl groups and organic ligands were the main adsorption sites, which captured $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ ions to the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes via the formation of Zr–O–As coordination bonds. One disadvantage of UiO-66 is the relatively slow adsorption process; the equilibrium of As(v) adsorption to UiO-66 required $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and no reusability studies of UiO-66 have been reported.
4.2.2 MOF-based materials for adsorption of both $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s(m)}$ . Additionally, ZIF-8 was also efficient for the simultaneous elimination of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ and $\mathrm{\bfAs}\left(\mathrm{\bf\Omega}\mathrm{\bf\Omega}\right)$ owing to the presence of hydroxyl and amine groups.166 Adsorption equilibria of ZIF-8 for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{m}})$ were achieved in only 7 and $^{13\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively, because of the highly porous structure with pore sizes ranging from 8 to $16{\overset{\circ}{\mathbf{A}}}.$ On the basis of the Langmuir model, the maximum adsorption capacities for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{\mathrm{\Pi}}}_{\mathrm{{III}}})$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ were determined to be 49.5 and $60\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The adsorption efficiency of ZIF-8 was not influenced by the presence of ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ but was notably affected by the presence of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ and $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ .166 Combined with the FTIR and XPS studies, the authors proposed that arsenic species were coordinated to the terminal hydroxyl and imidazole nitrogen groups.
Two kinds of magnetic MOF composites including $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\textcircled{\mathbf{2}}$ $\mathbf{MIL}–101^{306}$ and $\mathbf{CoFe_{2}O_{4}@M I L\cdot100(F e)^{139}}$ were recently studied in the simultaneous removal of As(III) and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ . The adsorption capacities of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{\mathrm{m}}})$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L^{-101}}$ were 80.0 and $121.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.306 $\mathbf{CoFe_{2}O_{4}@M I L-100}\mathbf{(Fe}$ ) was able to remove $0.1\mathrm{~mg~L}^{-1}$ As(v) and $\mathrm{\bfAs}\left(\mathrm{\mathrm{\bf~\underline{~}{~}{~I I I}}}\right)$ in $2\ \mathrm{min}$ with maximum adsorption capacities of $114.8~\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ and $143.6\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{A}s\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ (Fig. 12a and b) because of the mesoporous structure with an average diameter of $260~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . As shown in Fig. 12c and d, owing to the electrostatic repulsion and size exclusion influence of the MOF shell, the adsorption efficiency of this composite was not affected by the presence of inorganic ions ${{(\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}}}$ , $\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SiO_{3}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ , ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ ) or natural organic substances.139 The capture of $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ and $\mathbf{A}s\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ on this composite occurred via hydroxyl substitution with the formation of Fe–O–As bonds. The relatively high adsorption of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathbf{m}})$ was attributed to hydrogen bonding, which resulted in multi-layer adsorption of neutral $\mathrm{\bfAs}\left(\mathrm{\mathrm{\bf\Omega}_{\mathrm{III}}}\right)$ , whereas the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v})$ was a monolayer reaction process. Importantly, the magnetic properties of these composites could be harnessed for their practical applications.
4.2.3 MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s(m)}$ adsorption. ZIF-8 with three different morphologies (cubic, leaf-shaped and dodecahedral ZIF-8) were studied for As(III) adsorption.307 At $\mathsf{p H}8.5$ , the maximum adsorption capacities were 122.6, 108.1 and $117.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for cubic, leaf-shaped and dodecahedral ZIF-8, respectively. Up to $95\%$ of As(III) was removed by the three kinds of ZIF-8 within $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Interestingly, $\mathrm{As}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ adsorption capacity and kinetics of the non-porous leaf-shaped ZIF-8 (surface area of $\sim13\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ were almost the same as those of highly porous cubic and dodecahedral ZIF-8 (surface areas of 958 and $1151~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively).307 FTIR and XPS analysis revealed that the adsorbed $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{m})$ could substitute the terminal hydroxyl groups with the formation of Zn–O–As. Thus, $\mathbf{A}s(\mathrm{\mathrm{m}})$ adsorption by ZIF-8 nanoparticles was not affected by their surface area or morphology, but by the surface functional groups, which formed strong surface complexes with As(III).
MOF-based composites have also been applied as adsorbents for $\mathbf{As(m)}$ adsorption, in particular 1D ${\bf\beta p-M n O}_{2}@{\bf Z I F-8}$ composites, which were successfully synthesized by a simple and rapid method.90 The adsorption kinetics and isotherms of $\mathbf{A}s\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ onto this composite were well described by the PSO and Langmuir models, respectively. As shown in Fig. 13, the adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{As}(\mathrm{_{III}})$ on ${\upbeta}{\cdot}\mathbf{MnO}_{2}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}{-}\mathbf{8}$ was $140.27~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was about 1.6 times as high as that of pristine ZIF-8 nanoparticles $(90.8\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The high $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{\mathrm{\mathbf{m}}})$ adsorption on ${\upbeta}{\cdot}{\bf{M n O}}_{2}{\ @}{\bf{Z I F}}{\cdot}{8}$ was attributed to the oxidation ability of $\mathsf{\beta}_{\mathsf{{P}}\mathsf{{M n}}\mathsf{{O}}_{2}}$ nanowires in the core. The resulting oxidized $\mathbf{A}s(\mathbf{v})$ could be adsorbed via electrostatic attraction to ZIF-8. Importantly, $\scriptstyle\left\{3\cdot\mathbf{MnO}_{2}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}\cdot8\right.$ could be easily separated from water after settling for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , while ZIF-8 nanoparticles remained suspended in the aqueous solutions.90 Further investigations are needed on the adsorption efficiency with varying pH, excellent selectivity and reusability.
4.2.4 MOF-based materials for organoarsenic adsorption. Jhung’s group has reported extensively on the adsorption of organoarsenic species by MOF-based materials.149,176,308 In 2015, researchers in this group compared the $p$ -arsanilic acid (ASA) adsorption capacities among AC, zeolite $\mathbf{Y}$ and various MOFs including ZIF-8, mesoporous ZIF-8, MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-53(Cr).176 The authors found that mesoporous ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 were more efficient than AC. The adsorption difference among these materials was not solely dependent on their structural properties. The main mechanism of ASA adsorption onto mesoporous ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 was electrostatic interaction at $\mathsf{p H}<9$ , and one reason was given for mesoporous ZIF-8 being more efficient than ZIF-8. Another study from this group assessed the adsorption of ASA and ROX over different porous materials including MIL-53(Cr), MIL-100(Al), MIL-100(Cr), MIL-100(Fe), MIL-101(Cr), AC, zeolite Y and goethite.149 There were two major findings: (i) MIL-100(Fe) showed the highest adsorption capacity; and (ii) MIL-100(Al) and MIL-100(Cr) displayed similar adsorption capacities. MIL-100(Al), MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-100(Fe) were further studied to understand the influence of the central metal ion of MIL-100 on the adsorption performance. The authors found that the adsorption difference among MIL-100 analogues can be traceable to the water adsorption energy and the organic molecular adsorption energy. Water was shown to bind much more weakly to MIL-100(Fe) than to other MIL-100 analogues. Additionally, ASA and ROX coordinated more strongly to MIL-100(Fe) than to other MIL-100 analogues. The authors suggested that the anionic species of ASA and ROX were bound to coordinatively unsaturated sites (CUSs) of the MIL-100(Fe). In 2017, researchers in this group compared the adsorption efficiency of phenylarsonic acid (PAA) and ASA onto functionalized MIL-101 (MIL-101(OH) and $\mathbf{MIL}–101(\mathbf{OH})_{3}]$ with pristine MIL-101 and commercial AC.308 The adsorption capacities of PAA and ASA decreased in the following order: $\mathrm{\bfMIL^{-101}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}>\mathrm{\bfAC\>\MIL^{-101}}(\mathrm{OH})> $ MIL-101. Hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions were proposed to contribute to the adsorption of PAA and ASA onto these materials.
Additionally, MOF-based materials have also been studied for the adsorption of organoarsenic species.100,149,176 These MOFs show superior adsorption efficiencies over a wide pH range and can be easily regenerated and reused several times. In terms of removal efficiency, UiO-66 with changeable missing-linker defects was found to be more promising for organoarsenic adsorption than MIL100(Fe). Notably, MIL-100(Fe) showed high adsorption capacities in both batch experiments and column studies. Some MOFs have been investigated for As(III) elimination with adsorption capacities ranging from 49.5 to $147.28~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .90,139,166,306,307 Some other cost-effective adsorbents have also been applied to $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathrm{m})$ adsorption.320–323 However, their capacities were relatively low $\left(\leq20\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}\right)$ . Costeffective Fe–Cu binary oxides $(122.3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})^{324}$ and $\mathbf{Mg}_{0.27}\mathbf{Fe}_{2.50}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ $\left(127.4~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)^{325}$ showed comparable adsorption capacities.
In addition to adsorption capacity and adsorption kinetics, the effects of antimony (Sb) on adsorption of arsenic species should also be taken into consideration for practical applications. Arsenic usually occurs together with Sb in water around old mining and smelting areas and they share numerous similarities in their chemical properties and toxicity.326,327 Table 6 shows several MOF-based materials, which display excellent selectivity for arsenic species in the presence of other inorganic anions.100,139,147,177,188 As a well-known competitor for the adsorption of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{s}(\mathbf{v}),$ , the effects of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{-}$ required further investigations.
## 4.3 Mercury ions
${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ is a widespread environmental pollutant that is particularly hazardous even in very low concentrations.1,52 Recently, MOFs have attracted considerable attention for applications to ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ decontamination. In the following subsection, we reviewed ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption by MOF-based materials via coordination and ion-exchange mechanisms, respectively.
4.3.1 Binding with sulfur atoms. Owing to the strong-binding ability between sulfur atoms and a wide range of metal ions, the incorporation of highly reactive sulfur atoms and sulfurcontaining functional groups into MOFs has been widely investigated for the efficient elimination of metal ions,78,328–331 particularly for ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption. To date, several thiolfunctionalized or thioether-modified MOFs have been investigated for elimination of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ ions.78,109,328,332,333 Notably, Zhou et al. first investigated a 3D MOF for reversible adsorption of $\mathrm{\bfHgCl}_{2}$ .332 Analytical results disclosed that one mole of $\mathrm{\mathrm{Hg}C l}_{2}$ could insert per formula unit of the MOF. Strong interactions between $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ and the framework of the MOF were favoured because of the presence of the soft thioether functional groups in the MOF.332 Shortly after, Pardo’s group reported a 3D BioMOF showing an extremely high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{\bfHgCl}_{2}$ $(900~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})^{89}$ and they found that $\mathbf{HgCl}_{2}$ adsorption onto this 3D BioMOF was preceded by a two-step process that involved initial extremely fast adsorption of three molecules of $\mathrm{\bfHgCl}_{2}$ per formula unit and a second slower adsorption process to reach a final loading of up to five molecules. This BioMOF was also able to remove $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{HgCl}$ with an adsorption capacity of $166~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .89 Recently, this group reported another thioether decorated MOF (MOF 1), which was water-stable, environmentally friendly and could be easily synthesized on a large scale at low cost.333 Owing to the high affinity of sulphur atoms for mercury, MOF 1 showed a high selectivity for $\mathrm{\mathrm{Hg}C l}_{2}$ under interference from various cations including NaCl, $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CaCl}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{AlCl}_{3}$ , and ${\mathrm{FeCl}}_{3}$ (Fig. 14a). MOF 1 could extract up to $99.7\%$ of $\mathrm{\bfHgCl}_{2}$ in only $5\mathrm{min}$ and decreased the concentration of $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ to within permissible limits for drinking water in 10 min (Fig. 14b). The authors attributed the excellent adsorption performance of MOF 1 to its structural characteristics. Specific coordinative interaction between sulphur atoms and $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ resulted in the very stable tetrahedral conformation of the resulting $\mathrm{\mathrm{Hg}C l}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ adduct (Fig. 14c). We also note another example of a sulfur-functionalized MOF (FJI-H12) for ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption.108 In this study, $\mathbf{NCS}^{-}$ groups were introduced into the metal cluster of MOFs in situ via coordination bonds. FJI-H12 was able to selectively remove $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ from water with a moderate adsorption capacity $\left(439.8\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ ) owing to the strong coordination interactions between $\mathbf{SCN}^{-}$ groups and $\mathrm{Hg(\mathrm{II})}$ ions as evidenced by FTIR analysis.108 The adsorption kinetic data were in good agreement with the PSO model. Furthermore, FJI-H12 could be obtained on a large scale under mild conditions. More interestingly, a continuous and fast removal of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ from water has been realized with the use of a column loaded with FJI-H12 microcrystals.108
4.3.2 Binding with other functional groups and ion exchange. Except for binding with sulfur, $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ can also bind with carbonyl, acylamide and hydroxyl groups in the MOFs.106,174 For example, acylamide and hydroxyl functionalized MOFs showed an adsorption capacity of $\sim250\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ towards $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ according to the Langmuir model.106 In addition, kinetic data fitted well with the PSO model and revealed that ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption by this adsorbent required only $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to reach equilibrium even at initial $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g}$ concentration as low as $100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . A combination of characterization techniques including FTIR, XPS, XRD, SEM, and EDX were applied to probe the possible adsorption mechanisms, which revealed that ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ was inserted as $\mathrm{\bfHgCl}_{2}$ . Such adsorption properties were determined from the striking structural characteristics of its 1D regular hexagonal channel with a pore size of $\sim5.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and numerous acylamide and hydroxyl groups. However, this MOF was efficient over a limited pH range (4–7). No selectivity or reusability properties were reported for this material. Recently, thymine-functionalized MIL-101 (MIL-101- thymine) also showed adsorption ability for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ . The isotherm data could be described by the Langmuir model with a maximum adsorption capacity of $51.27\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The adsorption kinetic data were in good agreement with the PSO model. It was found that MIL-101-thymine exhibited excellent selectivity towards $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ over other cations owing to the highly selective interactions of $\mathrm{T}{\cdot}\mathrm{Hg}^{2{+}}{\cdot}\mathrm{T}$ in MIL-101-thymine and cations. XPS and TGA results disclosed that one $\mathrm{Hg}$ atom was coordinated with two N atoms in thymine. Furthermore, MIL-101-thymine was also able to remove trace amounts of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ from real water samples, and satisfactory recoveries were achieved. However, the working pH range was limited, and the maximum adsorption capacity was only $51.27\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, $34\%$ of the adsorption capacity was lost after only three times of recycling processes.105
$\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ adsorption via ion exchange was reported for only one MOF (AMOF- $1^{\prime}\bar{}$ ).334 The maximum adsorption capacity for ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ was $78\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ owing to the anionic features of this MOF. Inductively coupled plasma analysis revealed that the number of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ ions per formula unit was 0.31 after ion exchange for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which increased to 0.48 after 7 days. Nearly complete removal $\left(98.7\%\right)$ of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ at an initial concentration of $1\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ was achieved within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which is crucial for real applications.
4.3.3 Comparison between MOF-based materials and other adsorbents. Table 7 tabulates ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption data for reported MOF-based materials. A combination of a highly porous structure and well-distributed functionalities are collectively crucial in effective decontamination of ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ ions. Notably, UiO-66-NHC(S)NHMe,110 $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{BTC})_{2}]_{n}^{109}$ and 3D bioMOFs89 showed exceptionally high adsorption capacities (769, 714 and $900~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively). Such adsorption capacities are comparable to those of thiol functionalized mesoporous silica $(635~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,335 and modified covalent organic frameworks (COFs) $\left(734\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}\right.$ for TAPB-BMTTPA- $\mathrm{COF}^{336}$ ), and are much higher than those of MSIEs $\left(377~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ for KMS-1),36 resins $363~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Duolite GT-73),337 and mesoporous silica $(179\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})\$ .338 Other adsorbents such as thiol modified organic polymers $\mathrm{1014~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{PAF}–1–\mathrm{SH}^{3.39}$ and $1216~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for POP$\mathbf{SH}^{340}$ ) and post-synthetically modified COFs $(1350~\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})^{341}$ showed relatively high adsorption capacities; however, the preparation of COFs requires costly organic ligands; the synthesis of PAF-SH1 and POP-SH requires multistep reactions including modification of porous organic polymers. Many MOF-based adsorbents showed excellent selectivity and reusability,111,134,155,342 which are vitally important characteristics for practical applications. MOF 1 and FJI-H12 are environmentally friendly and can be easily prepared on a large scale at low cost, which suggests realistic possibilities for commercialization and practical applications.333
Mercury forms complexes in real wastewater systems and is rarely found in the ionic form. Therefore, a study of the efficiency of MOF-based adsorbents using industrial wastewater is of high priority.
## 4.4 Other heavy metal ions
4.4.1 MOF-based materials for the adsorption of selenium oxyanions. Selenium oxyanions are naturally occurring elements that primarily exist as selenite $\left(\mathrm{SeO_{3}}^{2-}\right)$ and selenate $\left(\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}\right)$ in water. Low concentrations of selenium are essential for human health ( $\cdot<40~\upmu\mathrm{g}$ per day), and the difference between the dietary deficiency and toxicity ${\mathit{\Omega}}^{r}{>}400~\upmu\mathrm{g}$ per day) is narrow.343 The maximum level for selenium in drinking water mandated by the U.S. EPA is 50 ppb.344 To date, only a few studies have focused on the adsorption of selenium oxyanions by MOFbased materials.
Howarth et al. applied a series of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -based MOFs to remove selenate and selenite from solutions, and found that NU-1000 displayed the highest adsorption capacities (102 and $62~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for selenite and selenate respectively) and the fastest adsorption kinetics.77 The porous structural properties of the large pore size (12 and $30\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ ) resulted in fast adsorption kinetics. The high adsorption capacity may be attributed to the abundance of aqua and hydroxy groups on the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes. However, no direct correlation between the adsorption capacity and porosity was observed. X-ray total scattering data and diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy analyses revealed that selenite and selenate ions exclusively bound to the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes in a $\boldsymbol{\ \eta}_{2}\boldsymbol{\upmu}_{2}$ fashion. Our group recently studied the adsorption of selenate by $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ MOFs designed with different kinds of defects via a modulator synthetic strategy.98 XPS results revealed that the displaceable ligands (aqua and hydroxy groups) and weak-binding ‘‘capping’’ ligands $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{O_{2}C-C H_{3}}^{-}$ , ${\bf O}_{2}{\bf C}{-}{\bf C}{{\bf F}_{3}}^{-}$ or $\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{CHF}_{2}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{HO}^{-}$ and/or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{\mathcal{I}}^{\mathrm{Y}}$ ) on $\mathrm{zr}_{6}$ nodes could be substituted by Se(VI) oxyanions via a mechanism of anion-exchange. More efficient and faster uptake of $\mathrm{\mathop{Se}(v)}$ oxyanions was obtained with increased densities of both missing linker defects and missing cluster defects. Experimental and theoretical results disclosed that smaller $\mathbf{HO}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ compensating ligands could be more easily substituted than the $\mathbf{O_{2}C-C H_{3}}^{-}$ , ${\bf O}_{2}{\bf C}{-}{\bf C}{{\bf F}_{3}}^{-}$ or $\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{CHF}_{2}^{-}$ compensating ligands. We found that the adsorption capacity of UiO-66-HCl $\left(86.8\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}\right)_{,}$ was higher than that of NU-1000 $\left(62\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right).$ , disclosing that the modulator synthetic strategy is highly useful for expanding the pore size of MOFs and introducing defects that act as binding sites. This approach can be used to take full advantage of the potential MOFs as adsorbents.
Materials that have been reported as potential adsorbents for $\mathrm{seO_{3}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{seO_{4}}^{2-}$ anions include MOFs,77,98 clay materials,345,346 metal oxides and their composites,347–349 LDH and intercalated LDH,350 functionalized silica materials and their resins,351–353 and GO-based materials.354 Among these materials, NU-1000 displayed a comparable adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{seO}_{3}^{2-}$ $(95~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ 1 to that of MgO nanosheets $(103.52\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,347 while Gly-LDH had a much higher adsorption capacity for $\operatorname{seO}_{4}^{2-}$ $(208.6~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .350 Interestingly, NU-1000 showed the fastest adsorption kinetics for $\mathrm{seO_{3}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{seO_{4}}^{2-}$ $\mathrm{(<1\min)}$ . Furthermore, NU-1000 showed essentially equal efficiency for both $\mathrm{SeO_{3}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , a property that has been achieved by few other adsorbents. In terms of practical applications, UiO-66-HCl showed a high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{seO_{4}}^{2-}$ and represents a promising option owing to its low cost and easy preparation. However, further investigations on the reusability, selectivity, and column studies of NU-1000 and UiO-66-HCl are required before real application.
4.4.2 MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption. Heavy metal ions such as $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ are non-biodegradable and highly toxic to living organisms even at low concentrations, which also have a tendency to accumulate in the environment and living organisms.1,52 The efficient removal of these toxic heavy metal ions is of vital importance for human beings and environmental protection.
A typical example of MOFs applied to eliminate $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is the ED-MIL-101.104 Its maximum adsorption capacity was $81.09\mathrm{\mg\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as determined by batch methods. This value is five times as high as that of pristine MIL-101, and the adsorption equilibrium required only $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . ED-MIL-101 was also highly effective for the treatment of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ -contaminated practical water samples with high selectivity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions in the presence of other competitive divalent metal ions (e.g., $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ). Such high selectivity was attributed to the strong complexation of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions by EDTA, and the fitting of the metal radius to the pore structure of MIL-101. This MOF can also be reused after three or four cycles with little loss in the adsorption capacity. Recently, magnetic composites based on amino-functionalized MIL-53(Al) showed a higher capacity (up to $492.4~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ for $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions than that of pristine MIL-53(Al) mainly attributed to the nitrogen-based functional groups.114 The authors found that the adsorption capacity could be elegantly tuned by changing the concentration of $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ groups in this composite (Fig. 15a). The regeneration of this composite could be partially realized by magnetic heating (Fig. 15b), although studies on the reusability of the regenerated adsorbent were not performed. The adsorption energy (2.6 eV) calculated by DFT calculations revealed the strong interactions between $\mathrm{{\bfPb}({\mathrm{\bfn}})}$ atoms and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups (Fig. 15c). PXRD results revealed that $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions were sequestered into the pores, which induced the formation of an open form phase that enhanced with the increase of $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}b(\mathrm{_{II})}}$ concentration (Fig. 15d). A thorough understanding, via both the experimental results and DFT calculations, of the adsorption mechanism is required to understand the interaction mechanism in more detail at the molecular and atomic levels.
To date, several MOF-based materials have been reported for the elimination of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions from aqueous solutions (Table 8); however, detailed mechanistic investigations by experimental approaches and computational calculations remain scarce. The MOF-based composite with the highest $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorption capacity is MCNC@Zn-BTC $\left(558.66~\mathrm{\mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ ,356 which was much higher than those of MSIEs $(319\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ for KMS-1),36 resins $\mathrm{238~\mg~\g^{-1}}$ for Duolite GT-73),357 modified nanocellulose $(53\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ ,358 and clays and functionalized clays $365~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Mg-MTMS,359 $19~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{PCB}^{360}$ ). However, the most selective adsorbent is magnetic thiol-functionalized $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ $\left(K_{\mathrm{d}}=1.23\times10^{4}\mathrm{mLg}^{-1}\right)$ .111 NZVI showed exceptional adsorption capacity for $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ $(1667~\mathrm{~mg~}~\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{_{II}})$ per g Fe);361 however, the small particle size and powder properties restrict the direct application of NZVI. Moreover, NZVI particles tend to aggregate, thus restricting the direct use of NZVI for field scale applications.
4.4.3 MOF-based materials for $\mathbf{Cd}^{2+}$ adsorption. Very few MOFs have been reported for $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ adsorption to date. Typically, the sulfonic acid-functionalized HKUST-1 $(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{3}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{TC})_{2}–\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H})$ showed a relatively low adsorption capacity $(88.7\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})^{138}$ but fast kinetics with a rate constant of $0.6818\mathrm{\g\min\mg^{-1}}$ (i.e., 1–3 orders of magnitude higher than those of other adsorbents). $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ adsorption by $\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{BTC})_{2}–\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ was not affected by interfering cations $\left(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\right)$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ ) mainly because of the functionalization of $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ . In addition, this $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ modified MOF was active over a wide pH range (3–8) and could be reused for more than six cycles without any obvious loss in the adsorption capacity.138 Such excellent adsorption performance was attributed to chelation between $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions and the $-S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ groups. Additionally, regenerative FJI-H9 was studied for $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ adsorption and showed a relatively high adsorption capacity of $225~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and excellent selectivity in the presence of various background metal ions $\left(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+},~\mathrm{Hg}^{2+},~\right.$ $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions).180
Analysis of PXRD and EXAFS data showed that such high adsorption performance could be attributed to the unusual synergy of active sites and the confined cavity. Additionally, in situ reconstruction of used FJI-H9 was successfully achieved by immersing the used sample in KSCN solutions at ambient temperature.
There have been few studies of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ adsorption by MOFbased materials. Typical adsorption capacities range from 49 to $225~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table 8), which are less than those of KMS-1 $\left(329\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ and CMS $\mathrm{515~mg~g^{-1}})$ .36 Nevertheless, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{\big(BTC\big)}_{2}-$ $\mathrm{so}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ was active over a wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range and displayed fast adsorption kinetics, excellent selectivity and reusability. Thus, the exploration of MOF-based materials with high adsorption efficiency for $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ is worthwhile.
4.4.4 MOF-based materials for ${\bf C u}^{2+}$ adsorption. The first MOF used for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ adsorption was ZIF-8, which showed fast kinetics and an ultrahigh adsorption capacity $(\sim800\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}})$ ). ZIF-8 was efficient for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ adsorption over a pH range of 3–6 at low and high $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ concentrations. The adsorption mechanism included (i) ion exchange at low initial ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ concentrations; and (ii) coordination between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and the nitrogen groups on 2-methylimidazole at high $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ concentrations. Recently, Margariti et al. prepared a new Ca-MOF and discovered that ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ was adsorbed via exchange with the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ions.163 The adsorption kinetics and isotherm data were well described by the PSO and Langmuir models. The maximum adsorption capacity was determined to be only $68\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ by the batch technique, and a similar adsorption capacity was achieved in column experiments. Furthermore, the adsorption performance could be maintained over a wide pH range (3–8).163 This study represents the first case where the constituent metal ions of a MOF could be exchanged by adsorbate ions.
As tabulated in Table 8, relatively few MOF-based materials have been investigated for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ elimination. Among these, ZIF-8 showed the highest ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ adsorption capacity $(\sim800~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,186 which is superior to those of AC,362 natural zeolite,363 GO-based adsorbents $\mathrm{18~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{3}$ and $24.4\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{PD}/\mathrm{GO}^{364})$ , organic resins $(13-60\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})^{365}$ and LDH composites $\left(75.01\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right) $ .5 Although Ca-MOF showed a lower adsorption capacity $(68\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ than ZIF-8, its excellent reusability and wide active $\mathsf{p H}$ range are superior to those of the abovementioned adsorbents.
4.4.5 MOF-based materials for the simultaneous adsorption of various cations. To date, several MOF-based materials have been applied to simultaneously remove different cations.94,110,137,164,366 For example, $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ modified with thiophosgene, diphosgene and various isothiocyanates showed excellent adsorption capacities for heavy metal cations $\left(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\right.$ ， $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+})$ .110 UiO-66-NCS showed better extraction performance for heavy metal ions than UiO-66-NCO, which was attributed to the higher complexation ability of sulphur groups for soft metal cations. In particular, UiO-66-NHC(S)NHMe showed maximum adsorption capacities of 49, 117, 232 and $769\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ respectively, owing to the high affinity of sulfur for heavy metal ions.110 The features of the simple synthesis method and high chemical stability of UiO families show promise for further applications in the treatment of industrial waste effluents. Shortly after, $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -functionalized Zr-MOFs were also used for uptake of both $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ .164 The adsorption capacities of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ were 166.74 and $177.35~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at an initial concentration of $40\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}$ , respectively. The adsorption kinetics fitted well to the PSO model, and FTIR and XPS analyses disclosed the coordination interaction between the $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ or $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions and the amino groups $\left(-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ . TMU-5 was able to uptake trace amounts of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ owing to the high basicity of its azine groups.137 A 3D MOF was able to remove $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ within $80~\mathrm{{min}}$ ; for ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , the highest adsorption efficiency was obtained after $100~\mathrm{\min}$ and the adsorption capacities were in the order of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}>\mathbf{Al}^{3+}>\mathbf{Cd}^{2+}>$ $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g}^{2+}>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}^{2+}$ .366 However, this order has not been explained. These studies have laid the foundation for the successful application of MOF-based materials to the simultaneous removal of various cations.
Table 8 also presents the adsorption data of other heavy metal ions on various MOF-based materials. Many of these studies focused on adsorption of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , with limited reports on extraction of other heavy metal ions. The information about the competitive adsorption of coexisting heavy metal ions in most investigations was not provided, although it is of vital importance for practical applications.
## 5 Conclusions and prospects
In summary, we provide a comprehensive review of recent developments in MOFs and MOF-based materials for the removal of toxic/radioactive metal ions. We examined analyses of adsorption performance and approaches to mechanistic investigations, including batch experiments, modern advanced spectroscopy techniques, and theoretical calculations. We also assessed the adsorption behaviours of MOFs and their composites with other traditional and advanced artificial nanomaterials. MOFs have emerged as an extensive class of crystalline materials with ultrahigh porosity (up to $90\%$ free volume) and enormous internal surface areas (ranging from 1000 to $10000~\mathrm{~m~}^{2}~\mathrm{~g~}^{-1})$ as well as stable framework structures. As adsorbents for the removal and pre-concentration of toxic/ radioactive metal ions, MOFs and MOF-based materials showed various attractive characteristics, such as fast adsorption kinetics, high adsorption capacity, superior selectivity and excellent reusability; however, certain important information is missing from reports. Many MOFs perform well under harsh conditions, and outperform many other traditional and advanced adsorbents. We conclude that the considerable adsorption capacities of MOFs and MOF-based composites mainly resulted from interactions between target ions and functional binding groups on the MOFs, and together with the highly ordered porous structure, which can be controlled to facilitate diffusion of metal ions. Introducing appropriate functional groups into MOFs and tuning their porosity or modifying the pores of MOFs with isoreticular or post-synthetic strategies have been proven to be efficient strategies for increasing their selectivity and adsorption capacity for toxic/radioactive metal ions.
Despite great progress achieved in recent years, there remains a number of challenges and unsolved issues: (i) in general, the solution conditions (such as, pH, ionic strength, existence of competitive ions and organic matters, etc.) have a considerable effect on the surface properties of MOFs and on the adsorption behaviours of toxic/radioactive metal ions; however, less detailed information is available in many of the known reports; (ii) the active pH range of some MOFs is typically very narrow (e.g $\mathrm{:\pH\4.5-5.5}$ for MIL-101-DETA), thus the stabilization of more MOFs over a wider pH range is highly desirable in future research; (iii) compared with traditional materials, the adsorption kinetics of many MOFs are slow (taking dozens of hours to reach equilibrium) and require improvement; (iv) more water-stable MOFs should be explored for possible practical applications; (v) studies on the effects of radiation on the stability of MOFs have been scarce, and more information would be desirable; (vi) many MOFs are derived from expensive ligands, thus more economical alternatives are in great demand; (vii) the long-term stability of MOFs and regeneration represent a challenge in terms of secondary pollution and practical applications, thus MOFs that are resistant to structural degradation induced by moisture, oxidants/ reductants, acids/bases, and radiation are preferred for future research; (viii) most reported porous MOFs are microporous (with pore sizes less than $2\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ), and the augmentation of channel sizes or cavities of MOFs to the mesoporous range poses a great challenge. The extension of ligands for stable MOFs is a promising strategy; however, the framework interpenetration should be inhibited for guest metal ions entering internal pores; (ix) simultaneously competitive adsorption of multiple metal ions to MOFs is of importance for practical applications and requires more intensive investigations in the future.
Thus, more detailed investigations are required into the adsorption behaviours and mechanisms of MOFs and MOFbased materials as adsorbents. The stability, high adsorption capacity, superior selectivity, excellent reusability, and synthesis of MOFs and MOF-based materials through large-scale, low-cost and environmentally friendly methods should also be considered for practical applications of these materials in pollution management.
